<BaseLayout title="Example B: outline of submissions" description="A complete fictional respondent's outline of submissions for a Fair Work Commission unfair dismissal hearing, organised by section 387.">
  <main id="top" class="standalone" data-pagefind-body>
    <p class="eyebrow">Advocacy guide · Example B</p>
    <h1>Example B: an outline of submissions</h1>
    <p class="lead">A complete respondent's outline in the same fictional matter as <a href="/advocacy/witness-statement">Example A</a>. It is organised by the matters in s.387, and every fact is tied to a witness statement or an attachment.</p>
    <div class="notice">
      <strong>Fictional facts, real authorities.</strong> The employer, the people and the events are invented. The paragraph references to Ms Nair's statement match Example A. The statements of Mr Ashby and Ms Kwan are not reproduced, so references to them are illustrative. The decisions cited are real, and each quoted phrase has been checked against the decision; they are used for the propositions stated and no more. Do not copy an authority into your own outline without reading it. Check the current text of s.387 before following this structure.
    </div>

    <h2>What to notice</h2>
    <ul class="compact">
      <li><strong>The overview says who, what and why in three paragraphs.</strong> A Member who reads nothing else knows the case.</li>
      <li><strong>Agreed matters are cleared away early</strong>, so the outline spends its length on what is in dispute.</li>
      <li><strong>Facts come before argument</strong>, each with a reference.</li>
      <li><strong>Headings are propositions</strong>, and they follow the order of s.387. Every paragraph of the section gets an answer, including the ones that do not apply.</li>
      <li><strong>The other side's best point is met directly</strong> in Part D.3, with evidence first and authority second.</li>
      <li><strong>Authorities are few</strong> and each is used for one stated point.</li>
      <li><strong>Remedy is dealt with in the alternative</strong>, briefly, without conceding liability.</li>
    </ul>

    <div class="specimen">
      <p class="spec-head">IN THE FAIR WORK COMMISSION</p>
      <p>Matter No: U2026/0000<br />Applicant: Daniel Reeve<br />Respondent: Calder Coachlines Pty Ltd</p>
      <p class="spec-title">RESPONDENT'S OUTLINE OF SUBMISSIONS</p>
      <h3>A. Overview</h3>
      <p class="np"><span>1.</span><span>On 12 March 2026 the Respondent (<strong>Calder Coachlines</strong>) dismissed the Applicant (<strong>Mr Reeve</strong>) from his employment as a bus driver. It did so because, on 3 March 2026, he refused a direction to take part in a random drug and alcohol test and abused the collector and his supervisor.</span></p>
      <p class="np"><span>2.</span><span>Mr Reeve applies for an unfair dismissal remedy. Calder Coachlines says the application should be dismissed. There was a valid reason for the dismissal. Mr Reeve was told of the reason and given an opportunity to respond before the decision was made. No other matter makes the dismissal harsh, unjust or unreasonable.</span></p>
      <p class="np"><span>3.</span><span>There is no dispute that Mr Reeve was dismissed, that he was protected from unfair dismissal, or that the application was made in time. The only question is whether the dismissal was harsh, unjust or unreasonable, having regard to the matters in s.387 of the <em>Fair Work Act 2009</em> (Cth).</span></p>
      <h3>B. The evidence</h3>
      <p class="np"><span>4.</span><span>Calder Coachlines relies on three witness statements.</span></p>
      <p class="np sub"><span>•</span><span><strong>Sarah Nair</strong>, Depot Manager, who made the decision to dismiss. She gives evidence about the drivers' work, the Drug and Alcohol Policy, the process she followed and her reasons.</span></p>
      <p class="np sub"><span>•</span><span><strong>Thomas Ashby</strong>, Operations Supervisor, who directed Mr Reeve to attend for testing. He gives evidence about what was said on 3 March 2026.</span></p>
      <p class="np sub"><span>•</span><span><strong>Helen Kwan</strong>, a collector employed by Sentinel Workplace Testing. She gives evidence about how employees were selected for testing and what Mr Reeve said to her.</span></p>
      <p class="np"><span>5.</span><span>References below are to the witness and paragraph number, for example “Nair [14]”, and to attachments by their marking, for example “SAN-2”.</span></p>
      <h3>C. Factual background</h3>
      <h4>C.1 The policy</h4>
      <p class="np"><span>6.</span><span>Mr Reeve drove buses carrying up to 70 passengers, including school services, and worked without direct supervision: Nair [10].</span></p>
      <p class="np"><span>7.</span><span>Calder Coachlines has had a Drug and Alcohol Policy in its current form since 1 July 2024. It provides for random testing of drivers by an external provider. Clause 6.3 provides that refusing a test when directed is a serious breach which may result in dismissal: Nair [11]; SAN-2.</span></p>
      <p class="np"><span>8.</span><span>Mr Reeve completed refresher training on the policy on 14 January 2026 and signed an acknowledgment that he had read and understood it: Nair [14]; SAN-3.</span></p>
      <p class="np"><span>9.</span><span>Selection for testing is made by the external provider from the day's roster. Depot management does not choose who is tested and is not told beforehand: Nair [13]; Kwan [6]–[9].</span></p>
      <h4>C.2 The events of 3 March 2026</h4>
      <p class="np"><span>10.</span><span>At sign-on at about 5.40 am on 3 March 2026, Mr Ashby told Mr Reeve he had been selected and directed him to attend the testing room. Mr Reeve refused: Ashby [8]–[10].</span></p>
      <p class="np"><span>11.</span><span>Mr Ashby told him that refusing was a breach of the policy and could cost him his job, and repeated the direction. Mr Reeve refused again and left the building: Ashby [11]–[14]; TEA-1.</span></p>
      <p class="np"><span>12.</span><span>In the course of refusing, Mr Reeve used abusive language to Ms Kwan and to Mr Ashby. The words are set out at Kwan [12] and Ashby [10], [13].</span></p>
      <h4>C.3 The process and the dismissal</h4>
      <p class="np"><span>13.</span><span>Mr Reeve was stood down on full pay that morning: Nair [21]; SAN-4.</span></p>
      <p class="np"><span>14.</span><span>On 4 March 2026 Ms Nair wrote to him setting out two allegations and the words alleged, warning that dismissal was a possible outcome, and inviting a written response, a meeting, or both, with a support person: Nair [22]–[25]; SAN-5.</span></p>
      <p class="np"><span>15.</span><span>On 9 March 2026 Mr Reeve attended a meeting with a union delegate as his support person. He admitted refusing the test. He said he felt targeted because he had been tested in November 2025. He said there was no medical or other reason he could not provide a sample. He accepted he had “probably” used words like those alleged: Nair [28]–[30]; SAN-6.</span></p>
      <p class="np"><span>16.</span><span>Ms Nair considered his response, his service and his record. On 11 March 2026 she decided to terminate his employment. She told him on 12 March 2026 and confirmed it in writing, with payment in lieu of notice: Nair [34]–[41]; SAN-7.</span></p>
      <h3>D. There was a valid reason for the dismissal: s.387(a)</h3>
      <h4>D.1 The principles</h4>
      <p class="np"><span>17.</span><span>A valid reason is one that is <Cite id="[2026] FWC 218" at="[28]" phrase="sound, defensible or well founded" quote />. The Commission <Cite id="[2026] FWC 218" at="[31]" phrase="will not stand in the shoes of the employer" quote />; the question is whether the reason was a valid one, not whether the Commission would have made the same decision.</span></p>
      <p class="np"><span>18.</span><span>Where the reason is a refusal to be tested, the issue is whether the employee <Cite id="[2021] FWC 5677" at="[11]" phrase="refused a lawful and reasonable direction that she undertake a drug test" quote />. In <em>Desmond v Lyndons</em> the Commission proceeded on the basis, drawn from Full Bench authority, that <Cite id="[2021] FWC 5677" at="[15]" phrase="random and targeted drug testing is a reasonable and legitimate employer response to the risk to safety posed by employee drug use" quote />.</span></p>
      <p class="np"><span>19.</span><span>The same decision records the Full Bench's statement that <Cite id="[2021] FWC 5677" at="[12]" phrase="a substantial and wilful breach of a policy will often, if not usually" quote /> be a valid reason for dismissal, although each case turns on its own facts.</span></p>
      <h4>D.2 Mr Reeve refused a lawful and reasonable direction</h4>
      <p class="np"><span>20.</span><span>The refusal is admitted: Nair [28]; SAN-6.</span></p>
      <p class="np"><span>21.</span><span>The direction was lawful. Mr Reeve's contract required him to comply with company policies and lawful directions (SAN-1, clause 9), and the policy provided for random testing (SAN-2, clause 4).</span></p>
      <p class="np"><span>22.</span><span>The direction was reasonable. Mr Reeve drove passenger vehicles on public roads, alone: Nair [10]. Random testing by an external provider is a proportionate way of managing the risk that a driver is impaired.</span></p>
      <p class="np"><span>23.</span><span>Mr Reeve knew the rule and the consequence. He was trained on the policy seven weeks earlier (SAN-3), and Mr Ashby told him twice on the morning that refusing was a breach: Ashby [11], [13].</span></p>
      <h4>D.3 Mr Reeve had no reasonable basis for refusing</h4>
      <p class="np"><span>24.</span><span>Mr Reeve's explanation is that selection was not truly random because he had been tested in November 2025. That should not be accepted, for three reasons.</span></p>
      <p class="np sub"><span>•</span><span>First, the evidence is that Sentinel made the selection from the day's roster without input from the depot: Kwan [6]–[9]; Nair [13]. That evidence is not contradicted.</span></p>
      <p class="np sub"><span>•</span><span>Second, a similar argument failed in <em>Desmond</em>, where the Commission accepted that selection from a list generated without notice to site management was random within the meaning of the policy: <Cite id="[2021] FWC 5677" at="[142]" phrase="I also accept that the Applicant was randomly selected for testing on 1 February 2021" quote />.</span></p>
      <p class="np sub"><span>•</span><span>Third, even if Mr Reeve had a genuine concern, the policy required him to take the test and raise the concern afterwards: SAN-2, clause 6.5; Nair [37]. He gave no medical or other reason: Nair [28]–[29].</span></p>
      <p class="np"><span>25.</span><span>In <em>Desmond</em> the Commission found that the employee <Cite id="[2021] FWC 5677" at="[152]" phrase="refused to comply with a lawful and reasonable direction to undergo a urine test on 1 February 2021 and that she had no reasonable basis for the refusal" quote />, and that the dismissal was not unfair. The same conclusion should be reached here.</span></p>
      <h4>D.4 The abuse</h4>
      <p class="np"><span>26.</span><span>Mr Reeve did not deny the language alleged: Nair [30]. The evidence of Ms Kwan and Mr Ashby about it is first-hand and consistent. Ms Nair treated it as making the refusal more serious, not as a separate ground: Nair [40].</span></p>
      <h3>E. Notification and opportunity to respond: s.387(b) and (c)</h3>
      <p class="np"><span>27.</span><span>Notification of the reason must come before the decision to dismiss: <Cite id="[2023] FWC 3059" at="[58]" phrase="Procedural fairness requires that an employee be notified of a valid reason for their termination before any decision is taken to terminate their employment" quote />. It must be in plain and clear terms: <Cite id="[2024] FWC 3017" at="[37]" phrase="explicitly putting the reasons to the employee in plain and clear terms" quote />.</span></p>
      <p class="np"><span>28.</span><span>The letter of 4 March 2026 set out each allegation, the words alleged and the possible outcome, a week before any decision was made: SAN-5; Nair [22]–[25].</span></p>
      <p class="np"><span>29.</span><span>Mr Reeve was given an opportunity to respond in writing and in person. He took it up at the meeting of 9 March 2026, which lasted about 40 minutes, and said he had nothing further to add: Nair [27]; SAN-6. Ms Nair considered what he said before deciding: Nair [34]–[38].</span></p>
      <h3>F. The remaining matters: s.387(d) to (g)</h3>
      <p class="np"><span>30.</span><span><strong>Support person (s.387(d)).</strong> Mr Reeve was told he could bring a support person and did so: SAN-5; SAN-6.</span></p>
      <p class="np"><span>31.</span><span><strong>Warnings about unsatisfactory performance (s.387(e)).</strong> This paragraph does not apply. Mr Reeve was dismissed for conduct, not performance.</span></p>
      <p class="np"><span>32.</span><span><strong>Size of the enterprise and human resources expertise (s.387(f) and (g)).</strong> Calder Coachlines is a medium-sized employer with a People and Culture team. Neither matter affected the procedure followed, and Calder Coachlines does not rely on either.</span></p>
      <h3>G. Other relevant matters: s.387(h)</h3>
      <p class="np"><span>33.</span><span><strong>Service and record.</strong> Mr Reeve had seven years of service and no prior warnings. Ms Nair took both into account and considered a final written warning: Nair [38]. She concluded it was not enough, for reasons she explains: Nair [39]. That was a conclusion open to her.</span></p>
      <p class="np"><span>34.</span><span><strong>The offer of a later test.</strong> An offer to be tested six days later does not answer a refusal on the day. The purpose of the test was to know whether Mr Reeve was fit to drive on 3 March: Nair [31].</span></p>
      <p class="np"><span>35.</span><span><strong>Consistency.</strong> The two other drivers who have refused a random test since July 2024 were also dismissed: Nair [42].</span></p>
      <p class="np"><span>36.</span><span><strong>Personal consequences.</strong> Calder Coachlines accepts that dismissal has serious consequences for Mr Reeve. That is to be weighed against the seriousness of a deliberate refusal by a driver of passenger vehicles. It does not make the dismissal harsh.</span></p>
      <h3>H. Conclusion and remedy</h3>
      <p class="np"><span>37.</span><span>For these reasons the dismissal was not harsh, unjust or unreasonable, and the application should be dismissed.</span></p>
      <p class="np"><span>38.</span><span>If the Commission finds otherwise, Calder Coachlines says reinstatement would be inappropriate. Ms Nair's evidence is that she could not again rely on Mr Reeve to comply with safety directions when unsupervised: Nair [43]. Calder Coachlines asks to be heard further on compensation, including on Mr Reeve's conduct and his earnings since dismissal.</span></p>
      <p>&nbsp;</p>
      <p>J Smith<br />For the Respondent<br />12 June 2026</p>
    </div>

    <h2>Decisions cited in this example</h2>
    <ul class="compact">
      <li><em>Desmond v Lyndons</em> [2021] FWC 5677, discussed in <a href="/modules/drug-and-alcohol">Module 11</a>.</li>
      <li><em>Potter v Cement Australia Holdings</em> [2026] FWC 218, cited only for its statement of the valid reason test; see <a href="/modules/valid-reason">Module 6</a>.</li>
      <li><em>Hickey v Mt Alexander Timber &amp; Hardware</em> [2023] FWC 3059 and <em>McDonald &amp; Phelps v Northern Rivers 4WD</em> [2024] FWC 3017, discussed in <a href="/modules/notification-and-response">Module 7</a>.</li>
    </ul>

